            'title' => 'Mechanisms of Neural Reorganization After Stroke',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Following a left-hemisphere stroke that damages perisylvian language areas, the brain undergoes a staged process of reorganization. In the acute phase, diaschisis -- a temporary suppression of function in regions connected to but distant from the lesion -- can make initial deficits appear larger than the lesion alone would predict. As edema resolves over the first weeks, some function returns simply because diaschisis resolves, not because of true relearning.</p><p>Beyond this acute recovery, two longer-term mechanisms dominate the literature: perilesional reorganization, in which tissue adjacent to the damaged area assumes language functions previously handled by the lesioned tissue, and right-hemisphere recruitment, in which homologous regions in the right hemisphere become more active during language tasks. Evidence from fMRI studies suggests that perilesional recruitment is generally associated with better long-term outcomes, while heavy reliance on right-hemisphere regions -- particularly right inferior frontal gyrus -- is sometimes associated with less complete recovery, possibly because these regions are less specialized for the fine-grained phonological and syntactic processing the left hemisphere typically performs.</p><p>These findings have direct clinical implications: therapy that encourages perilesional engagement, through tasks that stress the spared left-hemisphere network, may yield more durable gains than approaches that passively allow right-hemisphere compensation to dominate.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'If right-hemisphere recruitment is sometimes linked to poorer outcomes, should clinicians actively try to suppress or redirect it during therapy, or does this framing oversimplify a genuinely compensatory process? Defend your position with reference to lesion site and severity.']],
