        [
            'title' => 'Constraint-Induced Aphasia Therapy and Intensity Principles',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Constraint-Induced Aphasia Therapy (CIAT), adapted from Constraint-Induced Movement Therapy used in motor rehabilitation, is built on the principle that forcing use of a impaired function -- rather than allowing compensation through a spared modality -- drives greater neural reorganization. In CIAT, clients are restricted from using gesture, writing, or pointing and must communicate verbally during structured card-based language games, even when speech is effortful.</p><p>CIAT is typically delivered in a "massed practice" format: several hours per day across consecutive days, rather than the traditional one-hour-per-week outpatient model. This reflects the dosage principle emerging from animal and human studies of neuroplasticity: synaptic strengthening and reorganization require a threshold level of repetition and intensity within a bounded time window, and sparse, infrequent practice may fall below that threshold.</p><p>Meta-analyses comparing CIAT to conventional therapy show modest but consistent advantages for CIAT on standardized aphasia measures, particularly for chronic aphasia where spontaneous recovery has plateaued. However, the forced-use constraint is not appropriate for every client -- those with severe apraxia of speech or significant comprehension deficits may become frustrated rather than engaged, underscoring that intensity principles must be balanced against individual tolerance and the risk of learned non-use in the opposite direction.</p>']],
