    'modules' => [
        [
            'title' => 'The AAC Landscape: Unaided, Aided, Low-Tech, High-Tech',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Augmentative and Alternative Communication (AAC) encompasses any tool or strategy, beyond natural speech, that supplements or replaces spoken communication. The field divides broadly into unaided AAC, which requires no external material -- manual sign, gesture, and facial expression -- and aided AAC, which involves an external tool, ranging from a simple picture card to a sophisticated speech-generating device (SGD).</p><p>Within aided AAC, the low-tech versus high-tech distinction matters clinically. Low-tech systems, such as communication boards, PECS (Picture Exchange Communication System) books, or alphabet boards, require no power source, are inexpensive, and are never subject to battery failure or software crashes, making them indispensable as backup systems even for clients who primarily use high-tech devices. High-tech systems, including dedicated SGDs and tablet-based apps with text-to-speech output, offer larger vocabularies, dynamic screen displays, and voice output that can support participation in group settings and with unfamiliar communication partners.</p><p>No single system fits all clients, and many successful AAC users employ a multimodal approach, combining a high-tech device for novel or lengthy messages with gesture, vocalization, and low-tech backups for speed and reliability. Clinicians should resist the assumption that "more advanced technology" is automatically "better"; the right system is the one that matches the client\'s motor, cognitive, linguistic, and environmental profile.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'A family insists their nonspeaking child should be given a high-tech SGD immediately because "that is what modern AAC looks like," while the evaluating clinician recommends starting with a low-tech core board. How would you navigate this disagreement, and what evidence would you bring to the conversation?']],
            ],
