        [
            'title' => 'Core Vocabulary and Vocabulary Selection',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Core vocabulary refers to the relatively small set of high-frequency words -- primarily verbs, pronouns, prepositions, and other function words such as "go," "more," "stop," "want," "that," and "not" -- that account for a disproportionate share of everyday communication across contexts. Research on language sampling consistently shows that roughly 80% of what both children and adults say in daily interaction is built from a core vocabulary of only a few hundred words, with the remaining "fringe vocabulary" (nouns like "dinosaur" or "birthday cake") being highly context-specific and individually variable.</p><p>This has reshaped AAC board design: rather than building topic boards filled with nouns for narrow situations (a "zoo" board, a "snack" board), many current approaches establish a stable core vocabulary display that stays constant across contexts, supplemented by fringe vocabulary folders or pages for specific topics. A stable core layout allows motor planning to become automatic over time, since the location of "want" or "more" never changes, which is a critical consideration for clients who rely on procedural motor memory to navigate a device efficiently.</p><p>Selecting core vocabulary requires balancing generativity (how many different messages a word can help construct) against the client\'s current comprehension and motor access capabilities, and should be guided by language sampling of typical peers and the individual\'s own communication attempts wherever possible.</p>']],
