        [
            'title' => 'PECS and Structured Aided Communication Approaches',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>The Picture Exchange Communication System (PECS), developed by Bondy and Frost, is a structured protocol originally designed for children with autism who had limited or no functional speech. PECS proceeds through defined phases: in Phase I, the child learns the basic exchange -- handing a single picture to a communication partner in order to receive a desired item, establishing the foundational concept that communication is an exchange with social consequence. Later phases introduce distance and persistence, discrimination between multiple pictures, sentence structure using a "I want ___" strip, and eventually attributes, answering questions, and commenting.</p><p>PECS is notable for being one of the few AAC approaches with a substantial evidence base from randomized and quasi-experimental studies, showing gains not only in requesting behavior but, in some studies, in spontaneous speech production for a subset of learners, though this secondary effect is inconsistent across the literature and should not be oversold to families as a guaranteed outcome.</p><p>A common implementation error is treating PECS purely as a picture-pointing system rather than following its structured exchange and prompting-hierarchy fidelity, including the use of physical prompting and prompt fading rather than verbal cueing, which research suggests is important to the system\'s effectiveness.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'case_study', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>A 4-year-old boy with autism spectrum disorder has no functional spoken words and currently communicates primarily through tantrums and physical guidance of adults toward desired items. An AAC evaluation recommends beginning PECS Phase I. During initial sessions, the clinician notices the child will point at pictures but does not reliably initiate an exchange by picking up and handing over the picture independently, instead waiting for the adult to prompt each step.</p><p>This is a common early PECS challenge and reflects incomplete fading of physical prompts rather than a failure of the approach itself. Fidelity to the PECS protocol requires a second communication partner during initial teaching specifically to deliver physical prompts from behind the child (avoiding inadvertent verbal or gestural cueing from the front), with prompts systematically faded across trials. Rushing to Phase II (distance and persistence) before independent exchange is established in Phase I is a frequent implementation error that can stall progress.</p>', 'links' => [['url' => 'https://alzahra.institute/resources', 'reference' => 'Al Zahra Institute Resource Library']]]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Aided Language Stimulation and Partner Strategies',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>One of the most consistent findings in AAC intervention research is that the communication partner\'s behavior is as important as the AAC system itself. Aided Language Stimulation (ALS), also referred to as Aided Language Input or modeling, involves the communication partner pointing to or activating symbols on the AAC user\'s own system while simultaneously speaking, modeling the language the AAC user is expected to eventually produce. This mirrors how typically developing children acquire spoken language by hearing it modeled thousands of times before producing it themselves -- AAC users are frequently expected to "speak" through a system they have rarely seen modeled, an asymmetry ALS directly addresses.</p><p>Effective ALS does not require modeling every single word; research and clinical practice generally favor modeling key words within a natural utterance (for example, modeling just "go" and "more" while saying the full sentence aloud) rather than attempting to model entire sentences symbol-by-symbol, which can slow the interaction and reduce naturalness.</p><p>Partner training extends beyond modeling to include reducing the "question trap" (excessive testing questions like "what\'s this?") in favor of genuine communicative exchanges, allowing adequate response time given the motor and cognitive demands of AAC use, and presuming competence -- treating the AAC user as a capable communicator regardless of how limited their current output appears.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'research_activity', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'Observe a video-recorded or live interaction (with appropriate consent) between an AAC user and a communication partner, or review a published case study if direct observation is unavailable. Identify at least three specific partner behaviors that either supported or hindered aided language stimulation, and propose one concrete coaching point you would give the partner.', 'requires_submission' => true]],
            ],
