        [
            'title' => 'The Mental Lexicon and Lexical Access',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>The mental lexicon is the store of knowledge each speaker holds about the words of their language: their phonological form, meaning, grammatical category, and the contexts in which they typically appear. Unlike a printed dictionary, the mental lexicon is organized associatively, with words connected along multiple dimensions -- phonological similarity ("cat" and "cap"), semantic relatedness ("cat" and "dog"), and frequency of co-occurrence ("salt" and "pepper").</p><p>Lexical access refers to the process of retrieving a word\'s stored representation during comprehension or production. Experimental paradigms such as lexical decision tasks (deciding whether a letter string is a real word) and priming studies reveal that access is remarkably fast, typically within a few hundred milliseconds, and is influenced by word frequency -- high-frequency words like "house" are recognized faster than low-frequency words like "hovel" -- and by prior context, through a phenomenon called semantic priming, where exposure to "nurse" speeds recognition of the related word "doctor."</p><p>A key theoretical debate concerns whether lexical access proceeds serially, checking candidates one at a time, or in parallel, with multiple candidate words activated simultaneously and competing for selection. Most current models, including the Cohort Model and TRACE, favor parallel activation, in which several phonologically similar words ("candle," "candy," "candid") are briefly co-activated as a word is heard, before context and further acoustic information narrow the field to the intended target.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'If multiple phonologically similar words are briefly co-activated during listening, as the Cohort Model suggests, what would you predict happens when a listener hears the beginning of a word in a noisy environment where the end of the word is masked? Use cohort competition to justify your prediction.']],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Sentence Parsing and Garden-Path Sentences',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Garden-path sentences are a classic tool in psycholinguistics for revealing how the parser builds syntactic structure incrementally, word by word, rather than waiting until a sentence is complete. The canonical example, "The horse raced past the barn fell," initially leads readers to interpret "raced" as the main verb (as in "the horse raced past the barn"), only to encounter "fell" and realize that "raced" was actually part of a reduced relative clause meaning "the horse that was raced past the barn fell."</p><p>This phenomenon demonstrates that the parser commits to a structural analysis before all disambiguating information arrives, a property explained by competing theoretical accounts. The Garden-Path Model, associated with Lyn Frazier, proposes that the parser follows fixed structural preferences such as Minimal Attachment (building the syntactically simplest structure available) and Late Closure (attaching new material to the current clause rather than opening a new one), leading it "down the garden path" when these heuristics happen to produce the wrong analysis. Constraint-based models, by contrast, argue that multiple structural interpretations are activated in parallel and weighted probabilistically by frequency, semantic plausibility, and prior context, with garden-path effects occurring when a low-probability structure turns out to be correct.</p><p>Eye-tracking studies, which measure where and how long readers fixate as they process a sentence, show measurable processing slowdowns and regressive eye movements precisely at the disambiguating word, providing a real-time window onto the moment reanalysis occurs.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'practical_quiz', 'content' => ['questions' => [
                    ['question' => 'What do garden-path sentences demonstrate about sentence processing?', 'options' => [['text' => 'The parser waits until the end of the sentence before building any structure', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'The parser builds structure incrementally and can commit to an incorrect analysis before disambiguating information arrives', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'All sentence structures are processed with equal speed regardless of complexity', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Garden-path effects only occur in written, never spoken, language', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'The incremental commitment to a structural analysis, later revised, is exactly what produces the characteristic processing difficulty.'],
                    ['question' => 'What is "Late Closure" in Frazier\'s Garden-Path Model?', 'options' => [['text' => 'A preference for attaching new material to the clause currently being processed rather than opening a new one', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'A rule requiring sentences to end with a verb', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'A preference for the semantically simplest word meaning', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'The final stage of word retrieval in the mental lexicon', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Late Closure describes the parser\'s structural default of continuing the current clause rather than beginning a new syntactic unit.'],
                    ['question' => 'How do constraint-based models differ from the original Garden-Path Model?', 'options' => [['text' => 'They deny that garden-path effects exist', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'They propose multiple interpretations are weighted in parallel by probabilistic cues rather than following fixed structural rules', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'They apply only to spoken, not written, sentences', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'They reject the use of eye-tracking as a valid methodology', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Constraint-based accounts replace fixed heuristics with probabilistic weighting of frequency, plausibility, and context across competing parses.'],
                ]]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Models of Speech Production: Levelt\'s Framework',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Willem Levelt\'s model of speech production, elaborated with Roelofs and Meyer, describes language production as proceeding through a sequence of largely autonomous but interacting stages. The process begins with conceptualization, in which the speaker forms a preverbal message -- the communicative intention before it takes linguistic form. This feeds into formulation, which itself divides into grammatical encoding, selecting the appropriate lemma (an abstract word representation carrying syntactic properties such as word class) and building syntactic structure, and phonological encoding, retrieving the word\'s sound form (the lexeme) and assembling it into an articulatory plan.</p><p>A central piece of evidence for this staged architecture comes from speech errors. Semantic substitution errors, where a speaker says "fork" instead of the intended "spoon," suggest an error at the level of lemma selection, since the two words are semantically related but phonologically dissimilar. Phonological or "malapropism" errors, where a speaker says "comparable" for "compatible," suggest an error at phonological encoding, since the words are phonologically similar but semantically unrelated. The near-total absence of errors that are simultaneously semantically and phonologically related by chance supports the claim that these two levels of encoding are at least partially separable processing stages.</p><p>The final stage, articulation, converts the phonetic plan into actual motor commands to the vocal tract, while a self-monitoring loop allows speakers to detect and repair their own errors, evident in the frequency of self-corrections in natural speech.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'research_paper', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>A frequently cited line of evidence for Levelt\'s staged model comes from tip-of-the-tongue (TOT) studies, in which speakers temporarily fail to retrieve a word\'s phonological form despite having clear access to its meaning. Participants in TOT states can often correctly report the word\'s first letter, number of syllables, or grammatical gender (in languages that mark it), even while unable to produce the full phonological form. This dissociation is interpreted as evidence that lemma-level information (meaning, syntactic category) can be successfully retrieved while lexeme-level information (the full phonological form) temporarily fails, supporting the separation between grammatical and phonological encoding stages.</p><p>Critics of strict stage models point to interactive-activation accounts, which argue that semantic and phonological information can influence each other during production rather than proceeding in strict serial stages, citing evidence such as mixed errors (words that are both semantically and phonologically related to the target) occurring more often than chance in some experimental paradigms, suggesting at least some cascading or feedback between levels.</p>', 'links' => [['url' => 'https://alzahra.institute/resources', 'reference' => 'Al Zahra Institute Resource Library']]]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Applying Psycholinguistic Models to Real Language Behavior',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Psycholinguistic models are not purely theoretical; they inform how clinicians and educators understand breakdowns in real language use. A speaker who consistently produces semantic paraphasias (saying "chair" for "table") after a stroke may be experiencing difficulty at the lemma-selection stage described by Levelt\'s model, pointing clinicians toward therapy tasks that strengthen semantic-to-lexical mapping. A speaker who instead produces phonemic paraphasias (saying "tapple" for "table") points toward a breakdown at phonological encoding, suggesting a different therapeutic target.</p><p>Similarly, understanding that parsing is incremental and influenced by frequency and plausibility helps explain why certain sentence structures are reliably harder for language learners, individuals with aphasia, or children with developmental language disorder -- center-embedded relative clauses and other structures that require holding an incomplete syntactic analysis in memory impose demonstrably higher processing costs than simple linear structures, which is measurable and predictable rather than arbitrary.</p><p>This connection between laboratory psycholinguistics and applied communication disorders illustrates why the field sits at the intersection of cognitive science and clinical practice: the same mechanisms that explain a split-second lexical competition in a healthy adult also explain why a particular sentence structure derails comprehension for a specific client.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'Choose either a semantic paraphasia or a phonemic paraphasia example and trace, stage by stage through Levelt\'s model, exactly where you believe the breakdown occurred and why the other stages must therefore remain intact.']],
            ],
